you're watching book TV on c-span 2 this  weekend we're visiting Las Vegas Nevada  to talk with local authors and toured  the city's literary sites with the help  of our local cable partner Cox  Communications next we discuss sun-sin  and suburbia an essential history of  modern Las Vegas with author Jeff  Schumacher  so when people think about Las Vegas  they typically think about the Las Vegas  Strip right I mean think about the big  casinos they think about the shows they  think about gambling - think about the  restaurants they might think about  downtown Las Vegas where we have the  older casinos and we have Fremont Street  with all of the spectacle of the  promenade so that's what I think people  immediately think of when someone who  lives here someone who writes about Las  Vegas I don't necessarily think of that  first I think about the people the two  million people who live here I think  about the neighborhoods where I live and  where my friends and relatives live and  so much more I think nuanced and  complicated story than the typical  person from outside of town  imagine Las Vegas has been growing ever  since 1905 when it was you know founded  as a railroad town however there's  certain times certain decades when  things really accelerated and one of  those was certainly the 1940s and the  1940s you saw with during World War two  you saw the arrival here of a huge  magnesium production plant and this  production plant drew thousands and  thousands of workers to Las Vegas then  at the same time you had the development  of an army airfield which later became  an Air Force Base again thousands and  thousands of people descending on this  valley trip you know quadrupling the  population in a matter of years  coinciding with that was the development  of the casino business on a much larger  scale and those casinos again drew  workers they drew tourists and Las Vegas  became a a tourist place so the 40s were  huge the 50s is when you saw the rise of  all the iconic casinos on the Las Vegas  Strip that coincided with the rise of  atomic testing 60 miles north of Las  Vegas and so again a lot of scientists a  lot of others who needed to work at the  atomic testing site came and lived in  lost came to live in Las Vegas again  tourists also came to Las Vegas just to  witness these spectacles these  above-ground atomic explosions and so  the 40s and 50s I would have to say  where this really pivotal time  for las vegas and its growth las vegas  faced at a lot of different challenges  as it grew so quickly one was  transportation one another was education  building enough schools to accommodate  all of these kids a transportation for  example the building of the airport i  mean we were very late in getting caught  up with the demand and so the  development of McCarran Airport it was  opened and then suddenly you're already  talking about expanding expanding  expanding and then in the 50s you saw  the need to develop roads and you needed  to develop the infrastructure for the  spreading of the city and you know we  were always behind the curve Las Vegas  has never ever quite kept up with the  growth and that's probably the nature of  all fast-growing cities in the West but  Las Vegas has had an extreme case Chris  Wall the positive side keeping up with  growth in Las Vegas means there's lots  of jobs there's lots of people needed we  need manpower I need people to work in  the casinos we need them to work on the  construction we need them to work in the  different businesses around the  community so I'll see people coming here  for opportunities when they get here  oftentimes in the past there have been  you know school overcrowding that's a  big problem you find that there aren't  enough amenities restaurants are crowded  for example you find that the roads are  insufficient so people are living on  gravel roads that should otherwise be  paved and you're just this and then most  importantly you don't have enough tax  dollars you know to pay for all of these  infrastructure needs that you have so  then you're starting to look at raising  taxes raising property taxes raising  sales taxes etc in order to be able to  afford all of these new amenities so  again Las Vegas is constantly trying to  keep up with its growth in it and it has  really never let up there's a lot of  people who've been around Las Vegas for  a long time who would like to keep that  small-town feel that they remember here  from the earlier days unfortunately Las  Vegas has always been in a growth mode  and it there's never been a real sincere  attempt to control or manage  in Las Vegas we just you know we're  opportunists right we're just constantly  looking for the next opportunity whether  it's developing subdivisions or  commercial areas or casinos and so the  impact of the growth of the strip on the  suburbs has been this sort of insatiable  demand for more housing and when you  have more housing then you need more  schools and need more parks you need  more roads and you know we're basically  one big construction site you know and  that's been going on for decades now in  the past Las Vegas has had recessions it  suffered from slowdowns in its growth  you know periodically those national  recessions there's other kinds of  challenges that come up we had a water  issue in the early 90s that slowed up  the development for a while  but what happened in 2007 and 2008 was  almost unprecedented for Las Vegas  because we saw you know the real estate  market collapse here so dramatically and  then we saw the national recession that  hit with the wall street color crisis  and it just hammered Las Vegas tourism  so at that point between Satan 2007 and  2012 or 13 Las Vegas was in very sorry  shape you saw a lot of homes foreclosed  upon we saw people losing their jobs  high unemployment rate and you saw a lot  of vacant buildings not just houses but  commercial buildings and Las Vegas took  a long time to recover from that the  damage to the foreclosed upon homes by  squatters the people who get upset  because they're being evicted and they  tear up the house you saw this again and  again and again here you saw a lot more  homeless people on the streets you saw  people leaving town because they needed  to go find work somewhere or they lost  their home in the construction industry  the unemployment rate here was 50% so I  mean this is an amazing number for a  town that where people have grown  literally rich working blue-collar  construction work so you know it was a  very severe recession and it hit Las  Vegas as hard or harder than any place  in the country  the good news is the positive side is  Las Vegas is pretty resilient and it is  fighting back it is coming back the  growth is returning the economy is  growing people are getting back to work  and by 2016 you know things are starting  to look bright so I think the biggest  takeaway from sunset in suburbia is that  this is a community that has many more  facets than simply the strip the book  deals with the strip we have the history  of this strip I have the history of  downtown in the book it's very important  it's the crux of the whole enterprise  here but the the point of this book is  to for people who are local to kind of  understand where they live a little  better and if you're not local to  understand that this is a more complex  and interesting place than simply the  rows of slot machines along Fremont  Street or a long Las Vegas Boulevard  for more information on book TVs recent  visit to Las Vegas and many other  destinations on our city's tour
 

